### Coding Tips for AI engineers 

Covers everything from refactoring, readability, to testing, reproducibility, and collaboration (i.e. key elements to become production ready)
- Refactoring (i.e. changing structure and organisation of code for improved readability and scale but without changing substance or behaviour)
    - Keep functions short and focused (single responsibility)
    - Name things well (saves more time than comments)
    - Breakdown long scripts into modules (e.g. data_utils.py, modeling.py, viz.py)
    - Avoid code duplication: reuse functions 
    - Clean up old code after testing 
- Write Tests Early using frameworks such as unittest
    - Unit test your 
        - Feature engineering functions 
        - Model output shapes and ranges 
        - API endpoints (e.g. prediction services) 
- Organise project properly to help with reusability, teamwork and scaling by following standard structure such as 
my_project/ 
    - data/
    - notebooks/
    - src/ 
        - data.utils.py
        - modeling.py
        - inference.py
    - tests/
    - main.py
    - requirements.txt
    - README.md
- Write Clean, Documented Code 
    - Use docstrings for all functions """ insert_doc_string_here """
    - Add inline comments when necessary, but don't overload 
    - Use type hints for better readability and editor support 
        - E.g. def predict(x: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray: 
- Track Experiments Like a Scientist to ensure results are reproducible and reviewable 
    - Use MLflow or Weights & Biases to log 
        - Model params 
        - Metrics 
        - Dataset versions 
        - Git commit hashes 
- Use Git branches to Version Control everything 
    - Branches include 
        - main: production-ready
        - dev: in-progress
        - feature branches like feature/model-tuning 
    - Commit atomic, meaningful chunks of work with clear messages
    - Use .gitignore to avoid committing data, models, and logs 
- Think in terms of pipelines 
    - Break work into stages: ingest -> clean -> transform -> model -> serve 
    - Use DAG tools like Airflow or Prefect to schedule & track 
    - Aim for modular and composable code blocks 
- Automate boring stuff 
    - Use Makefile, nox, or tox for: 
        - Tests
        - Formatting (black, isort)
        - Linting (flake8, pylint)
    - Setup Github Actions to automate tests and checks on every push 
- Log, Monitor, and Visualize 
    - Use logging module - not just print()
    - Track input/output drift with tools like Evidently AI 
    - Built dashboards with Streamlist, Dash, or embed in notebooks 
- Respect Data Privacy & Ethics 
    - Never log or expose sensitive data
    - Build explainability into models (SHAP, LIME)
    - Regularly assess fairness, bias, and representativeness of data
